\paragraph{Safety training selectivity.}
The model$\times$type interaction (C4) reveals that safety training does not uniformly reduce ambiguity vulnerability. The universal vulnerability of incompleteness (46--62\%) is consistent with the absence of external disambiguation channels: when policy information is simply missing, no amount of safety training can compensate. By contrast, authorization scope involves explicit boundary reasoning that safety training can target through RLHF, and thus functions as a \emph{canary type} for safety training quality (\S\ref{sec:c4}).

\paragraph{Systematic Nocuity.}
Our finding that 41.3\% of ambiguous clauses trigger majority-convergent violations extends the nocuous/innocuous binary \citep{berry_kamsties_2004}: in \emph{systematic nocuity}, all models converge on the same wrong interpretation, making the error invisible in agreement-based metrics and arguably more dangerous than divergent nocuity.

\paragraph{Practical implications.}
Policy auditing should be \emph{type-aware}: incompleteness and authorization scope ambiguities should be prioritized, as they produce the highest and most model-dependent violation rates. Ambiguity detection must be \emph{externalized}: with fewer than 11\% self-awareness, agents cannot flag ambiguous clauses themselves; external detection tools \citep{chantree_2006,ferrari_ambiguity_2019} offer a more reliable triage mechanism.

